body:
@Haroenv had the same question. The reasoning behind this implementation is to keep the behavior consistent between primitives and managed parameters. Applied to widgets it helps to understand:

```js
search.addWidgets([
  index({ indexName: "topLevel" }).addWidgets([
    // Top level searchBox
    searchBox(),
    index({ indexName: "subLevel" }).addWidgets([
      // Sub level searchBox
      searchBox()
    ])
  ])
]);
```

The two widgets `search box` are independent from each other. Each widget **controls** its part of the state where it is mounted. The sub-level `searchBox` does not inherit from the top-level one, it overrides it. We apply the same rule to the managed parameters that are on the **same attribute**. Note that this rule only applies to managed parameters shared across multiple indices. Within the same index both widgets can read/write on the internal state.

With React InstantSearch I've never seen a use case where multiple widgets inherit from each other within a multi-index context. The behavior of React InstantSeach is the same as the one described above. I agree that it's a tradeoff but to it's the behavior that makes sense (IMO) with the idea of controlled/uncontrolled widgets.

